    {
      "name": "〜んでしょ (right?)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Combine the explanatory ん (n) with でしょ (desho, seeking confirmation). This creates a casual way to seek agreement — 'right?' or 'isn't it?'. It's the casual form of のでしょう (no deshou).\n\nExample from this song: 忘れてないんでしょ (wasuretenainddesho) = 'you haven't forgotten, right?' — from 忘れてない (wasuretenai, 'haven't forgotten') + んでしょ (ndesho, 'right?')\n\nMore examples:\n• 知っている (shitteiru, 'know') → 知ってるんでしょ (shitterundesho, 'you know, right?')\n• 好き (suki, 'like') → 好きなんでしょ (suki nandesho, 'you like it, right?')\n• 行きたい (ikitai, 'want to go') → 行きたいんでしょ (ikitaindesho, 'you want to go, right?')\n\nNote: んでしょ is softer than でしょ alone and adds emotional depth. It's very common in anime dialogue when a character confronts another about their true feelings."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "忘れてない (wasuretenai, 'haven't forgotten') + んでしょ (ndesho, 'right?') → 忘れてないんでしょ (wasuretenaindesho, 'you haven't forgotten, right?')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜ばっか / ばかり (just)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Place ばかり (bakari) or its casual form ばっか (bakka) after a past tense verb to mean 'just did' or after a noun to mean 'only/nothing but'. It indicates exclusivity or recency.\n\nExample from this song: 始まったばっか (hajimatta bakka) = 'just started' — from 始まった (hajimatta, 'started') + ばっか (bakka, 'just')\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べたばかり (tabeta bakari, 'just ate')\n• 文句 (monku, 'complaints') → 文句ばかり言う (monku bakari iu, 'do nothing but complain')\n• 来た (kita, 'came') → 来たばっかり (kita bakkari, 'just arrived')\n\nNote: た + ばかり = 'just did' (recency). ばかり with nouns = 'only/nothing but'. ばっか/ばっかり are casual contractions. 遊んでばかりいる (asonde bakari iru, 'do nothing but play')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "始まる (hajimaru, 'to start') → 始まった (hajimatta, past) → 始まったばっか (hajimatta bakka, 'just started')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜きれない (can't completely)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the verb masu-stem and add きれない (kirenai). This means 'can't do X completely' — the action can't be finished or fully accomplished.\n\nExample from this song: 隠しきれない (kakushi kirenai) = 'can't fully hide' — from 隠す (kakusu, 'to hide') → 隠し (kakushi, masu-stem) + きれない (kirenai)\n\nMore examples:\n• 食べる (taberu, 'to eat') → 食べきれない (tabe kirenai, 'can't eat it all')\n• 数える (kazoeru, 'to count') → 数えきれない (kazoe kirenai, 'too many to count')\n• 伝える (tsutaeru, 'to convey') → 伝えきれない (tsutae kirenai, 'can't fully express')\n\nNote: The positive きる (kiru) means 'to do completely': 走りきる (hashiri kiru, 'run the whole distance'). Negative きれない means the completion is impossible."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "隠す (kakusu, 'to hide') → 隠し (kakushi, masu-stem) + きれない (kirenai, 'can't completely') → 隠しきれない (kakushi kirenai, 'can't fully hide')"
    },
    {
      "name": "〜出す (begin to)",
      "jlpt_reference": "JLPT N3",
      "explanation": {
        "en": "How it works: Take the verb masu-stem and add 出す (dasu, 'to put out'). As an auxiliary, it means 'to suddenly begin doing'. It emphasizes the abruptness of the start.\n\nExample from this song: 動き出す (ugoki dasu) = 'begin to move' — from 動く (ugoku, 'to move') → 動き (ugoki, masu-stem) + 出す (dasu)\n\nMore examples:\n• 走る (hashiru, 'to run') → 走り出す (hashiri dasu, 'suddenly start running')\n• 降る (furu, 'to fall — rain') → 降り出す (furi dasu, 'suddenly start raining')\n• 泣く (naku, 'to cry') → 泣き出す (naki dasu, 'burst into tears')\n\nNote: Compare with はじめる (hajimeru, 'begin to') which is a gradual, expected start. 出す implies suddenness and often surprise. 急に笑い出した (kyuu ni warai dashita, 'suddenly burst out laughing')."
      },
      "conjugation_path": "動く (ugoku, 'to move') → 動き (ugoki, masu-stem) + 出す (dasu, 'to begin') → 動き出す (ugoki dasu, 'begin to move')"
